        "review": [
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "if you slice the onion and then soak it in ice water for about an hour it will \"bloom\" or open up so you can serve it like the Chilis and Outback do. The souce is great."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SHORECOOK"
            },
            "reviewBody": "YUM! Just like what you get at the Texas Roadhouse! I used a soup ladle to make the cuts. This kept the blade of the knife from cutting through and kept all the cuts the same depth. After cutting, I soaked the onion in ice water then using my thumb I separated each row of pedals under water. (It's cold but the water helps to keep the pedals separated) I used a bowl to contain the milk and egg but used a zip lock bag for the flour mixture. I found I could work the flour mixture into the pedals that way. A \"Fry Daddy\" was used for deep frying. The first one I made seemed difficult but Hubby wasn't home and wanted me to make another when he got home. (He saw the photo!) The second was much easier because I knew what I was doing. After making the second one, I decided I will make these again. The dip was perfect! Thank you Joanne Bruck for sharing!",
            "datePublished": "2013-01-28T15:43:58.510Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Helen Pogomom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Both the sauce and the onion are scrumptious. I also soaked the sliced onion in an ice bath for a bit over an hour before continuing and the onion did bloom or flower out perfectly. The only addition I would make to this wonderful recipe is to use a couple of toothpicks through the bottom of the onion to keep it from falling apart. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2010-08-14T06:32:23.237Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer Brown"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We loved it. I did soak it in ice water for 1 1/2 hours so that it \"bloomed\" but the dredge and sauce was on point!!!",
            "datePublished": "2021-01-17T23:30:49.943Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "PuffyDough5995"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Well the onion was great but the batter is too spicy for the wife and kiddos. Don't fret, I believe that I found the remedy by replacing the one and a half teaspoons of cayenne pepper with Smoked Paprika and then adding a little cayenne pepper back to taste about 1/4 teaspoon. I've seen and tried other recipes that don't use thyme but I think the thyme is a key ingredient the flavor just gives it a pop and makes it like you were sitting at the restaurant.",
            "datePublished": "2024-10-22T14:09:11.083Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "terrib962"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Instead of a big vat of hot oil, and especially since seed oils are a no-no to eat and ghee is so expensive, change it up. Use a batter for the onion pieces and spray with avocado oil and spread out on a cookie sheet and bake. I use chick-pea flour (GF).",
            "datePublished": "2025-03-09T02:38:36.958Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Angie Gibas"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made this tonight and it was great! Next time I will core the middle of the onion and pour the egg/milk mixture over so it soaks down between the onion. Def will make again! Great flavor! Served it with sriracha mayo! winner!",
            "datePublished": "2025-03-22T23:57:57.350Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Brenda Crow"
            },
            "reviewBody": "DE-LICIC-IOUS!! We didn’t have enough oil to make it with an entire onion, so we made onions straws. I simply cannot tell you HOW GREAT it was. I’d put this up against any restaurant’s recipe any day! Loved it. Thanks so MUCH for sharing! I don’t think we’ll ever have people over for football without this recipe!( POST pandemic, that is)",
            "datePublished": "2020-10-17T20:28:14.400Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DapperPoke2222"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used a white onion and a single container of vegetable oil, using a ladel to pour the oil over the top of the onion. This was a quick cheap street food and I think it would work well as a food truck because of the few inexpensive ingredients and the ease of preparation and the low cook time. I used red hot in my aioli and yum.",
            "datePublished": "2024-07-24T13:55:14.933Z"
          },
